## Goal

Prepare a reproducible M5 release-candidate evidence packet after independent beta work. This issue does **not** authorize v1.0 publication by itself.

**Status:** blocked on #112 · G5–G6

## Required evidence

- source commit and locked dependencies;
- artifact/package identities;
- Rust/CLI and consumer SDK versions;
- supported schema/profile versions;
- supported host/editor profiles;
- OS/runtime compatibility matrix;
- migration/deprecation rules;
- recovery/unknown-outcome runbook;
- security and authority boundaries;
- install/update/uninstall instructions;
- known losses/non-goals;
- independent beta results and unresolved blockers.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Clean-machine installation is reproduced from published candidate artifacts or the documented candidate distribution path.
- [ ] Rust and TypeScript/reference consumers run the same supported golden workflow.
- [ ] Supported host profiles pass their recorded smoke/reopen checks.
- [ ] Newer/unsupported schema versions fail explicitly.
- [ ] Migration and rollback are tested for at least one prior supported candidate where applicable.
- [ ] Recovery evidence from M3 is linked and still valid for the candidate build.
- [ ] Security review lists authority, signing, replay, local-path and host-execution limits.
- [ ] Every remaining release blocker has an owner and explicit accept/narrow/stop condition.
- [ ] Maintainer records a separate decision whether to proceed toward v1.0/public registry publication.

## Dependencies

- #111
- #112
- M3 and M4 accepted consumer/execution boundaries

## Exit

M5 is complete when a third party can install the candidate, reproduce the supported workflow and understand exactly what is and is not supported. Public v1.0/tag/registry publication remains a separate maintainer action.

## Goal

Evaluate whether independent users can complete the Storytelling workflow and whether the control-plane structure reduces revision/handoff errors without claiming benefits unsupported by the study design.

**Status:** blocked on #109 and #111 · G5–G6

## Target workflow

Use a realistic 60–90 second rough-cut task from approximately 30–50 selected photos/clips/narration where practical. Participants should not be maintainers or authors of the implementation.

Freeze the protocol, build, fixtures and scoring **before** the first measured session.

## Measures

Record separately:

- completion / abandonment;
- task time;
- missed required edits;
- unnecessary edits to protected material;
- stale-review or approval mistakes;
- unresolved/missing footage decisions;
- recovery incidents;
- user-rated clarity/control;
- whether the participant would use the workflow again.

Where a control condition is feasible, compare against direct editor/agent use using the same material and task. Do not infer time-saving when the design does not support it.

## Acceptance targets

Roadmap target:

- [ ] at least 3/5 participants complete the rough-cut workflow;
- [ ] capture follow-up evidence for whether at least two participants are willing/able to return for another project.

Additional gates:

- [ ] all participant evidence is anonymized and traceable to the frozen protocol/build;
- [ ] failures are preserved with original input and expected result;
- [ ] repeated failures at the same step are routed to named engineering issues rather than patched ad hoc during the study;
- [ ] maintainer records continue/narrow/stop after reviewing results.

## Dependencies

- #109
- #111
- accepted M3 recovery/execution path

## Exit

This issue produces product evidence, not a marketing claim. Any performance or quality claim must be limited to what the prospective study actually measured.
